int main() {
    int minAge = 18;
    int count = 0;

    auto cube = [](int x) { return x * x * x; };
    std::cout << cube(2) << ' ' << cube(5) << '\n';

    auto isAdult = [minAge](int age) { return age >= minAge; };
    minAge = 21;
    std::cout << std::boolalpha;
    std::cout << isAdult(15) << ' ' << isAdult(19) << '\n';

    auto tick = [&count]() { ++count; };
    tick();
    tick();
    tick();
    tick();
    std::cout << count << '\n';
    return 0;
}

steps.body:
`std::sort` takes an optional third argument: a **comparator** that answers "does `a` go before `b`?" Return `true` when it does.

```cpp
std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), [](int a, int b) { return a > b; });  // descending
std::sort(v.begin(), v.end(), std::greater<int>());                // same, from <functional>
```

To sort by one key and break ties with another, compare the first key and fall back to the second only when they are equal:

```cpp
auto byLength = [](const std::string& a, const std::string& b) {
    if (a.size() != b.size()) {
        return a.size() < b.size();
    }
    return a < b;   // same length: alphabetical
};
```

Take strings by `const&` so the comparator does not copy them on every comparison.

`std::sort` does not promise any order for elements the comparator calls equal. `std::stable_sort` does: equal elements keep their original order, which is the tool when the original order is meaningful.

> Gotcha: always use `<` or `>`, never `<=` or `>=`. A comparator must say `false` when `a` and `b` are equal. A `<=` comparator breaks that rule, which is undefined behavior: `std::sort` can read past the end of the vector and crash.

